1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,030
In nineteen eighty-six,

2
00:00:02,030 --> 00:00:05,651
a Boeing seven twenty-seven took off from

3
00:00:05,651 --> 00:00:06,710
Mexico City.

4
00:00:06,890 --> 00:00:09,837
Minutes later, one of its tires exploded

5
00:00:09,837 --> 00:00:11,458
inside the wheel well,

6
00:00:11,458 --> 00:00:14,110
and the plane was lost with everyone

7
00:00:14,110 --> 00:00:14,773
on board.

8
00:00:14,953 --> 00:00:17,879
The tire was filled with ordinary air.

9
00:00:18,059 --> 00:00:21,128
Today, the tires on every big airliner

10
00:00:21,128 --> 00:00:23,632
are filled with something else.

11
00:00:23,812 --> 00:00:24,021
Why?

12
00:00:24,201 --> 00:00:27,271
And why don't airplane tires explode when

13
00:00:27,271 --> 00:00:29,743
they slam into the runway at more

14
00:00:29,743 --> 00:00:32,513
than one hundred fifty miles an hour?

15
00:00:32,693 --> 00:00:36,286
Today, ten landing gear secrets hidden in

16
00:00:36,286 --> 00:00:37,337
plain sight.

17
00:00:37,687 --> 00:00:40,247
The landing gear is the only part

18
00:00:40,247 --> 00:00:42,651
of an airplane that has to work

19
00:00:42,651 --> 00:00:43,349
as a car,

20
00:00:43,349 --> 00:00:44,667
a shock absorber,

21
00:00:44,667 --> 00:00:47,072
a brake, and a crash structure,

22
00:00:47,072 --> 00:00:49,321
sometimes in the same second.

23
00:00:49,501 --> 00:00:52,104
It is folded away for the whole

24
00:00:52,104 --> 00:00:54,372
flight, frozen at altitude,

25
00:00:54,372 --> 00:00:56,556
and then it has to unfold,

26
00:00:56,556 --> 00:00:59,411
lock, and catch a machine that can

27
00:00:59,411 --> 00:01:01,343
weigh hundreds of tons.

28
00:01:01,523 --> 00:01:04,818
Almost every strange detail on it exists

29
00:01:04,818 --> 00:01:07,536
because something, at some point,

30
00:01:07,536 --> 00:01:08,442
went wrong.

31
00:01:08,792 --> 00:01:11,674
As always, every number in this video

32
00:01:11,674 --> 00:01:13,698
comes from a named source,

33
00:01:13,698 --> 00:01:16,969
the Federal Aviation Administration rules,

34
00:01:16,969 --> 00:01:18,993
official accident reports,

35
00:01:18,993 --> 00:01:20,706
and the manufacturers,

36
00:01:20,706 --> 00:01:22,965
and all of them are listed in

37
00:01:22,965 --> 00:01:24,211
the description.

38
00:01:24,391 --> 00:01:27,024
Let's start with the question in the

39
00:01:27,024 --> 00:01:29,730
title, and with what is really inside

40
00:01:29,730 --> 00:01:30,973
an airplane tire.

41
00:01:31,323 --> 00:01:34,689
Why are airliner tires filled with nitrogen

42
00:01:34,689 --> 00:01:35,863
instead of air?

43
00:01:36,043 --> 00:01:38,660
Because air contains oxygen,

44
00:01:38,660 --> 00:01:42,212
and oxygen can turn an overheated tire

45
00:01:42,212 --> 00:01:43,334
into a bomb.

46
00:01:43,514 --> 00:01:46,035
When brakes get extremely hot,

47
00:01:46,035 --> 00:01:49,228
the rubber inside the tire can release

48
00:01:49,228 --> 00:01:50,573
flammable gases.

49
00:01:50,753 --> 00:01:54,035
Mix those with oxygen under high pressure

50
00:01:54,035 --> 00:01:56,837
and heat, and the tire can explode.

51
00:01:57,017 --> 00:01:58,723
Nitrogen is inert.

52
00:01:58,903 --> 00:02:00,796
It does not feed a fire.

53
00:02:00,976 --> 00:02:04,495
So under United States certification rules,

54
00:02:04,495 --> 00:02:07,687
the tires on braked wheels of airliners

55
00:02:07,687 --> 00:02:11,697
heavier than seventy-five thousand pounds must be

56
00:02:11,697 --> 00:02:13,743
filled with dry nitrogen,

57
00:02:13,743 --> 00:02:15,871
or a gas with no more than

58
00:02:15,871 --> 00:02:17,508
five percent oxygen.

59
00:02:17,859 --> 00:02:20,919
That rule was written after Mexicana Flight

60
00:02:20,919 --> 00:02:21,701
nine forty.

61
00:02:21,881 --> 00:02:23,551
On March thirty-first,

62
00:02:23,551 --> 00:02:26,587
nineteen eighty-six, a brake on the left

63
00:02:26,587 --> 00:02:29,775
main gear of the Boeing seven twenty-seven

64
00:02:29,775 --> 00:02:31,901
was dragging during takeoff.

65
00:02:32,081 --> 00:02:33,793
It overheated one tire,

66
00:02:33,793 --> 00:02:36,771
which had been filled with ordinary air.

67
00:02:36,951 --> 00:02:39,811
The tire exploded in the wheel well,

68
00:02:39,811 --> 00:02:42,115
cut fuel and hydraulic lines,

69
00:02:42,115 --> 00:02:45,055
and started a fire that destroyed the

70
00:02:45,055 --> 00:02:46,564
airplane in flight.

71
00:02:46,744 --> 00:02:49,542
One hundred sixty-seven people died.

72
00:02:49,722 --> 00:02:51,367
About a year later,

73
00:02:51,367 --> 00:02:56,214
the Federal Aviation Administration required nitrogen in

74
00:02:56,214 --> 00:02:57,513
airliner tires.

75
00:02:57,862 --> 00:03:00,637
Nitrogen has a second advantage.

76
00:03:00,817 --> 00:03:03,517
Dry nitrogen carries no moisture,

77
00:03:03,517 --> 00:03:05,971
so it does not freeze into ice

78
00:03:05,971 --> 00:03:09,080
inside the tire at minus fifty degrees

79
00:03:09,080 --> 00:03:10,798
at cruising altitude,

80
00:03:10,798 --> 00:03:13,498
and it does not corrode the wheel

81
00:03:13,498 --> 00:03:14,807
from the inside.

82
00:03:14,987 --> 00:03:18,014
So the gas in an airplane tire

83
00:03:18,014 --> 00:03:19,931
is chosen for fire,

84
00:03:19,931 --> 00:03:22,252
for cold, and for rust,

85
00:03:22,252 --> 00:03:23,463
all at once.

86
00:03:23,813 --> 00:03:26,836
Why do airplane wheels have small metal

87
00:03:26,836 --> 00:03:28,619
plugs designed to melt?

88
00:03:28,799 --> 00:03:31,595
Because the most dangerous moment for a

89
00:03:31,595 --> 00:03:33,315
tire is not the landing.

90
00:03:33,495 --> 00:03:35,567
It is a few minutes after a

91
00:03:35,567 --> 00:03:38,099
hard stop, when all the heat from

92
00:03:38,099 --> 00:03:40,554
the brakes soaks into the wheel.

93
00:03:40,734 --> 00:03:42,938
The gas inside heats up,

94
00:03:42,938 --> 00:03:44,775
the pressure climbs,

95
00:03:44,775 --> 00:03:46,887
and the tire can burst.

96
00:03:47,067 --> 00:03:49,611
So the wheel has fusible plugs,

97
00:03:49,611 --> 00:03:52,482
small threaded plugs with a core of

98
00:03:52,482 --> 00:03:54,451
low melting point metal.

99
00:03:54,631 --> 00:03:56,755
If the wheel gets too hot,

100
00:03:56,755 --> 00:03:59,694
the core melts and the tire deflates

101
00:03:59,694 --> 00:04:01,981
slowly instead of exploding.

102
00:04:02,331 --> 00:04:05,558
A flat tire after an emergency stop

103
00:04:05,558 --> 00:04:07,125
is not a failure.

104
00:04:07,305 --> 00:04:09,128
It is the system working.

105
00:04:09,308 --> 00:04:12,469
Ground crews know that after a rejected

106
00:04:12,469 --> 00:04:16,116
takeoff, they should approach the wheels from

107
00:04:16,116 --> 00:04:17,900
the front or the back,

108
00:04:17,900 --> 00:04:19,521
never from the side,

109
00:04:19,521 --> 00:04:21,547
in case a tire or a wheel

110
00:04:21,547 --> 00:04:22,195
lets go.

111
00:04:22,375 --> 00:04:25,561
And the escaping nitrogen even helps cool

112
00:04:25,561 --> 00:04:26,416
the brakes.

113
00:04:26,596 --> 00:04:29,847
Some heavy jets also have brake temperature

114
00:04:29,847 --> 00:04:32,417
sensors, so the pilots can see the

115
00:04:32,417 --> 00:04:35,139
heat building up before it becomes a

116
00:04:35,139 --> 00:04:35,744
problem.

117
00:04:36,095 --> 00:04:37,708
The numbers are extreme.

118
00:04:37,888 --> 00:04:40,974
An airliner tire is inflated to around

119
00:04:40,974 --> 00:04:43,815
two hundred pounds per square inch,

120
00:04:43,815 --> 00:04:46,657
roughly six times the pressure of a

121
00:04:46,657 --> 00:04:49,012
car tire, and it has to carry

122
00:04:49,012 --> 00:04:50,230
many tons each.

123
00:04:50,410 --> 00:04:52,772
The main tires of a Boeing seven

124
00:04:52,772 --> 00:04:56,167
thirty-seven next generation are rated for two

125
00:04:56,167 --> 00:04:58,676
hundred twenty-five miles an hour.

126
00:04:58,856 --> 00:05:00,902
And yet the same tire might do

127
00:05:00,902 --> 00:05:03,494
a few hundred landings before it needs

128
00:05:03,494 --> 00:05:04,313
a new tread.

129
00:05:04,662 --> 00:05:07,256
Why does an airplane tire have only

130
00:05:07,256 --> 00:05:08,961
a few straight grooves,

131
00:05:08,961 --> 00:05:11,258
and no tread pattern like a car

132
00:05:11,258 --> 00:05:11,628
tire?

133
00:05:11,808 --> 00:05:14,788
Because an airplane tire does not need

134
00:05:14,788 --> 00:05:16,905
to grip in every direction.

135
00:05:17,085 --> 00:05:19,055
It mostly rolls straight,

136
00:05:19,055 --> 00:05:20,552
at very high speed,

137
00:05:20,552 --> 00:05:22,995
and its biggest enemy is water.

138
00:05:23,175 --> 00:05:25,975
The straight grooves around the tire give

139
00:05:25,975 --> 00:05:27,683
rain a channel to escape,

140
00:05:27,683 --> 00:05:29,937
so the tire stays in contact with

141
00:05:29,937 --> 00:05:32,191
the runway instead of riding on a

142
00:05:32,191 --> 00:05:33,148
film of water.

143
00:05:33,328 --> 00:05:35,499
That is called hydroplaning,

144
00:05:35,499 --> 00:05:37,437
and it is one of the main

145
00:05:37,437 --> 00:05:40,305
reasons planes slide off wet runways.

146
00:05:40,656 --> 00:05:43,581
The grooves are also a wear gauge.

147
00:05:43,761 --> 00:05:46,048
When the tire wears down to the

148
00:05:46,048 --> 00:05:47,450
bottom of a groove,

149
00:05:47,450 --> 00:05:48,556
it is replaced.

150
00:05:48,736 --> 00:05:51,228
And airliner tires are retreaded,

151
00:05:51,228 --> 00:05:54,097
meaning the worn rubber is removed and

152
00:05:54,097 --> 00:05:56,363
a new tread is bonded onto the

153
00:05:56,363 --> 00:05:58,779
old casing, often several times.

154
00:05:58,959 --> 00:06:01,390
The casing of an airliner tire is

155
00:06:01,390 --> 00:06:04,484
a heavily layered structure built to carry

156
00:06:04,484 --> 00:06:05,368
a huge load,

157
00:06:05,368 --> 00:06:07,946
so throwing it away after one tread

158
00:06:07,946 --> 00:06:09,199
would be a waste.

159
00:06:09,549 --> 00:06:11,232
Runways help too.

160
00:06:11,412 --> 00:06:14,683
Many runways are grooved across their width,

161
00:06:14,683 --> 00:06:16,988
with thin cuts in the concrete,

162
00:06:16,988 --> 00:06:19,144
so water drains out sideways.

163
00:06:19,324 --> 00:06:22,176
Between the tire grooves and the runway

164
00:06:22,176 --> 00:06:24,515
grooves, a heavy jet can stop on

165
00:06:24,515 --> 00:06:26,708
a wet runway that would make a

166
00:06:26,708 --> 00:06:27,440
car slide.

167
00:06:27,790 --> 00:06:30,283
Why is there a shiny chrome piston

168
00:06:30,283 --> 00:06:32,190
on every landing gear leg?

169
00:06:32,370 --> 00:06:34,824
Because that is the shock absorber,

170
00:06:34,824 --> 00:06:36,858
and it is filled with oil and

171
00:06:36,858 --> 00:06:37,490
nitrogen.

172
00:06:37,670 --> 00:06:40,212
It is called an oleo strut.

173
00:06:40,392 --> 00:06:42,412
When the airplane touches down,

174
00:06:42,412 --> 00:06:44,496
the piston is pushed up into the

175
00:06:44,496 --> 00:06:45,083
cylinder.

176
00:06:45,263 --> 00:06:48,366
The nitrogen is compressed and works as

177
00:06:48,366 --> 00:06:49,082
a spring.

178
00:06:49,262 --> 00:06:50,563
At the same time,

179
00:06:50,563 --> 00:06:53,240
oil is forced through a small hole,

180
00:06:53,240 --> 00:06:55,688
and that turns the energy of the

181
00:06:55,688 --> 00:06:57,064
landing into heat.

182
00:06:57,244 --> 00:06:59,720
A metal spring would bounce the plane

183
00:06:59,720 --> 00:07:00,925
back into the air.

184
00:07:01,105 --> 00:07:02,463
The oil does not.

185
00:07:02,813 --> 00:07:05,571
The basic design was patented by the

186
00:07:05,571 --> 00:07:08,943
British company Vickers in nineteen fifteen,

187
00:07:08,943 --> 00:07:11,701
borrowed from the recoil system of a

188
00:07:11,701 --> 00:07:12,008
gun.

189
00:07:12,188 --> 00:07:14,410
More than a hundred years later,

190
00:07:14,410 --> 00:07:17,466
the shock absorbers of the biggest airliners

191
00:07:17,466 --> 00:07:19,757
still work on the same principle.

192
00:07:19,937 --> 00:07:22,025
The shiny part you can see is

193
00:07:22,025 --> 00:07:24,688
the piston, and that is why mechanics

194
00:07:24,688 --> 00:07:26,416
keep it perfectly clean.

195
00:07:26,596 --> 00:07:28,868
A scratch on it can damage the

196
00:07:28,868 --> 00:07:31,216
seals and let the oil leak out.

197
00:07:31,567 --> 00:07:34,794
Big airliners take this idea further.

198
00:07:34,974 --> 00:07:38,467
A Boeing seven seventy-seven main gear has

199
00:07:38,467 --> 00:07:40,962
six wheels on a frame called a

200
00:07:40,962 --> 00:07:43,873
bogie, and the whole bogie can tilt

201
00:07:43,873 --> 00:07:45,952
as it touches the ground,

202
00:07:45,952 --> 00:07:49,278
so the load arrives gradually instead of

203
00:07:49,278 --> 00:07:50,276
all at once.

204
00:07:50,456 --> 00:07:53,653
Spreading the weight over more wheels also

205
00:07:53,653 --> 00:07:56,317
spreads it over more of the runway,

206
00:07:56,317 --> 00:07:58,676
so the pavement is not crushed.

207
00:07:58,856 --> 00:08:01,267
That is why the heaviest planes have

208
00:08:01,267 --> 00:08:02,339
the most wheels.

209
00:08:02,689 --> 00:08:05,060
Why is there a strange folding arm,

210
00:08:05,060 --> 00:08:06,687
like a pair of scissors,

211
00:08:06,687 --> 00:08:08,923
on the front of the landing gear?

212
00:08:09,103 --> 00:08:12,092
Because the shock absorber is a piston

213
00:08:12,092 --> 00:08:13,822
sliding in a cylinder,

214
00:08:13,822 --> 00:08:16,417
and a round piston can also spin.

215
00:08:16,597 --> 00:08:18,733
Without something to stop it,

216
00:08:18,733 --> 00:08:21,533
the wheels could twist sideways as the

217
00:08:21,533 --> 00:08:23,227
gear moves up and down.

218
00:08:23,407 --> 00:08:24,881
The scissor link,

219
00:08:24,881 --> 00:08:27,310
also called the torque link,

220
00:08:27,310 --> 00:08:30,086
lets the piston slide in and out

221
00:08:30,086 --> 00:08:32,428
but stops it from rotating.

222
00:08:32,608 --> 00:08:35,235
It keeps the wheels pointing exactly where

223
00:08:35,235 --> 00:08:35,986
they should.

224
00:08:36,337 --> 00:08:37,589
On the nose gear,

225
00:08:37,589 --> 00:08:39,727
the scissors do one more job.

226
00:08:39,907 --> 00:08:42,619
The steering motor turns the upper part

227
00:08:42,619 --> 00:08:43,523
of the strut,

228
00:08:43,523 --> 00:08:46,306
and the torque link passes that rotation

229
00:08:46,306 --> 00:08:47,627
down to the wheels.

230
00:08:47,807 --> 00:08:49,144
On some airplanes,

231
00:08:49,144 --> 00:08:52,338
the ground crew disconnects the torque link

232
00:08:52,338 --> 00:08:54,863
before towing, so the tug can turn

233
00:08:54,863 --> 00:08:58,131
the nose wheel without fighting the steering

234
00:08:58,131 --> 00:08:58,651
system.

235
00:08:58,831 --> 00:09:01,477
On others, a bypass pin does the

236
00:09:01,477 --> 00:09:02,221
same job.

237
00:09:02,401 --> 00:09:04,463
That is why you sometimes see a

238
00:09:04,463 --> 00:09:06,859
mechanic putting a pin near the nose

239
00:09:06,859 --> 00:09:08,322
wheel before pushback.

240
00:09:08,673 --> 00:09:10,924
There is a reason the torque link

241
00:09:10,924 --> 00:09:12,016
matters so much.

242
00:09:12,196 --> 00:09:15,101
If a wheel turns even slightly off

243
00:09:15,101 --> 00:09:16,981
line at landing speed,

244
00:09:16,981 --> 00:09:20,058
the tire is dragged sideways and can

245
00:09:20,058 --> 00:09:22,877
be destroyed in a single landing.

246
00:09:23,057 --> 00:09:25,658
It can also start a violent vibration

247
00:09:25,658 --> 00:09:28,610
called shimmy, where the wheel shakes from

248
00:09:28,610 --> 00:09:29,524
side to side.

249
00:09:29,704 --> 00:09:32,213
Many nose gears have a damper just

250
00:09:32,213 --> 00:09:33,025
to stop it.

251
00:09:33,205 --> 00:09:35,428
A few metal links and a damper

252
00:09:35,428 --> 00:09:37,949
keep tons of airplane rolling in a

253
00:09:37,949 --> 00:09:38,986
straight line.

254
00:09:39,337 --> 00:09:41,750
Why are the brakes of an airliner

255
00:09:41,750 --> 00:09:43,725
tested until they glow red?

256
00:09:43,905 --> 00:09:46,825
Because they have to stop the airplane

257
00:09:46,825 --> 00:09:48,900
in the worst possible case,

258
00:09:48,900 --> 00:09:51,512
a rejected takeoff at full weight,

259
00:09:51,512 --> 00:09:52,972
at the last moment.

260
00:09:53,152 --> 00:09:56,658
Before Boeing could certify the seven forty-seven

261
00:09:56,658 --> 00:09:59,233
eight, test pilots loaded it to nine

262
00:09:59,233 --> 00:10:01,880
hundred seventy-five thousand pounds,

263
00:10:01,880 --> 00:10:03,955
accelerated to takeoff speed,

264
00:10:03,955 --> 00:10:06,459
and stopped it with brakes that had

265
00:10:06,459 --> 00:10:08,605
been worn down to their limit.

266
00:10:08,785 --> 00:10:10,399
The wheels glowed red.

267
00:10:10,579 --> 00:10:13,435
Then nobody was allowed to touch them

268
00:10:13,435 --> 00:10:14,747
for five minutes.

269
00:10:15,098 --> 00:10:17,574
Those five minutes simulate the time it

270
00:10:17,574 --> 00:10:19,924
might take for airport fire trucks to

271
00:10:19,924 --> 00:10:20,368
arrive.

272
00:10:20,548 --> 00:10:22,850
The airplane has to get through that

273
00:10:22,850 --> 00:10:25,534
wait without the fire spreading beyond the

274
00:10:25,534 --> 00:10:25,982
wheels.

275
00:10:26,162 --> 00:10:27,432
In those tests,

276
00:10:27,432 --> 00:10:30,819
the fusible plugs we saw earlier usually

277
00:10:30,819 --> 00:10:33,105
melt and the tires go flat.

278
00:10:33,285 --> 00:10:34,480
That is expected.

279
00:10:34,660 --> 00:10:36,239
The airplane passed.

280
00:10:36,419 --> 00:10:39,210
And modern airliners use carbon brakes,

281
00:10:39,210 --> 00:10:42,001
stacks of carbon discs that are lighter

282
00:10:42,001 --> 00:10:45,007
than steel and can absorb enormous amounts

283
00:10:45,007 --> 00:10:45,580
of heat.

284
00:10:45,930 --> 00:10:48,240
Carbon brakes have a strange habit.

285
00:10:48,420 --> 00:10:50,457
They wear more from the number of

286
00:10:50,457 --> 00:10:52,495
times they are used than from how

287
00:10:52,495 --> 00:10:53,668
hard they are used.

288
00:10:53,848 --> 00:10:56,970
So pilots and airlines often use the

289
00:10:56,970 --> 00:10:58,358
brakes in fewer,

290
00:10:58,358 --> 00:11:02,694
firmer applications instead of many small touches.

291
00:11:02,874 --> 00:11:04,038
On the ground,

292
00:11:04,038 --> 00:11:06,366
after a long taxi or a heavy

293
00:11:06,366 --> 00:11:09,358
landing, the brakes can stay hot for

294
00:11:09,358 --> 00:11:10,356
a long time,

295
00:11:10,356 --> 00:11:14,263
and airlines track brake temperature before the

296
00:11:14,263 --> 00:11:15,344
next takeoff.

297
00:11:15,694 --> 00:11:18,489
Why don't airplane wheels lock up and

298
00:11:18,489 --> 00:11:20,906
skid when the pilots brake hard?

299
00:11:21,086 --> 00:11:25,382
Because airplanes had anti-lock brakes long before

300
00:11:25,382 --> 00:11:25,812
cars.

301
00:11:25,992 --> 00:11:28,442
In the early nineteen fifties,

302
00:11:28,442 --> 00:11:32,037
Dunlop in Britain introduced a system called

303
00:11:32,037 --> 00:11:32,691
Maxaret.

304
00:11:32,871 --> 00:11:35,235
It sensed when a wheel was starting

305
00:11:35,235 --> 00:11:36,720
to slow down too fast,

306
00:11:36,720 --> 00:11:38,949
which means it was about to lock,

307
00:11:38,949 --> 00:11:41,381
and released the brake for a moment.

308
00:11:41,561 --> 00:11:44,872
In tests, it cut stopping distances by

309
00:11:44,872 --> 00:11:46,702
about thirty percent,

310
00:11:46,702 --> 00:11:49,316
and it let jets land safely on

311
00:11:49,316 --> 00:11:50,361
icy runways.

312
00:11:50,711 --> 00:11:53,397
A locked wheel is a disaster on

313
00:11:53,397 --> 00:11:54,437
an airplane.

314
00:11:54,617 --> 00:11:57,624
The tire scrubs against the runway at

315
00:11:57,624 --> 00:12:00,631
high speed, wears through in seconds,

316
00:12:00,631 --> 00:12:01,525
and bursts.

317
00:12:01,705 --> 00:12:06,303
Maxaret also eliminated tire bursts from skids.

318
00:12:06,483 --> 00:12:10,113
Every airliner today has an anti-skid system,

319
00:12:10,113 --> 00:12:12,775
monitoring each wheel separately.

320
00:12:12,955 --> 00:12:15,916
And the technology went on to cars.

321
00:12:16,096 --> 00:12:19,175
One of the first cars with anti-lock

322
00:12:19,175 --> 00:12:21,143
brakes, the Jensen F F,

323
00:12:21,143 --> 00:12:24,478
used the Maxaret system from airplanes.

324
00:12:24,829 --> 00:12:26,496
On a modern airliner,

325
00:12:26,496 --> 00:12:30,308
the anti-skid works together with the autobrake.

326
00:12:30,488 --> 00:12:33,733
Before landing, pilots select how hard they

327
00:12:33,733 --> 00:12:34,714
want to stop.

328
00:12:34,894 --> 00:12:38,605
After touchdown, the system applies the brakes

329
00:12:38,605 --> 00:12:41,912
by itself, and the anti-skid adjusts each

330
00:12:41,912 --> 00:12:44,171
wheel many times per second.

331
00:12:44,351 --> 00:12:46,208
On a wet or icy runway,

332
00:12:46,208 --> 00:12:49,517
this is what keeps the airplane straight.

333
00:12:49,697 --> 00:12:52,669
The pilots feel smooth deceleration.

334
00:12:52,849 --> 00:12:56,076
Underneath, the brakes are pulsing on and

335
00:12:56,076 --> 00:12:56,390
off.

336
00:12:56,741 --> 00:12:58,777
Why is there a puff of smoke

337
00:12:58,777 --> 00:13:01,396
every time an airliner touches down?

338
00:13:01,576 --> 00:13:03,914
Because the wheels are not spinning when

339
00:13:03,914 --> 00:13:05,082
they hit the runway.

340
00:13:05,262 --> 00:13:07,765
The airplane is moving at more than

341
00:13:07,765 --> 00:13:10,054
one hundred fifty miles an hour,

342
00:13:10,054 --> 00:13:12,414
but the tires are standing still.

343
00:13:12,594 --> 00:13:14,308
In a fraction of a second,

344
00:13:14,308 --> 00:13:16,351
friction has to spin them up to

345
00:13:16,351 --> 00:13:17,076
full speed.

346
00:13:17,256 --> 00:13:18,655
During that moment,

347
00:13:18,655 --> 00:13:21,749
the rubber slides against the concrete and

348
00:13:21,749 --> 00:13:23,444
a thin layer burns off.

349
00:13:23,624 --> 00:13:24,886
That is the smoke,

350
00:13:24,886 --> 00:13:26,990
and the black marks you see at

351
00:13:26,990 --> 00:13:28,813
the start of every runway.

352
00:13:29,164 --> 00:13:31,808
So why not spin the wheels before

353
00:13:31,808 --> 00:13:32,449
landing?

354
00:13:32,629 --> 00:13:36,098
Engineers have tried the idea many times,

355
00:13:36,098 --> 00:13:38,806
with small fins or motors on the

356
00:13:38,806 --> 00:13:39,398
wheels.

357
00:13:39,578 --> 00:13:42,016
But it adds weight and complexity to

358
00:13:42,016 --> 00:13:44,522
every flight, and the rubber is cheap

359
00:13:44,522 --> 00:13:45,673
compared to that.

360
00:13:45,853 --> 00:13:48,180
Tires are simply designed to lose a

361
00:13:48,180 --> 00:13:49,975
little tread every landing.

362
00:13:50,155 --> 00:13:52,798
The black rubber is later removed from

363
00:13:52,798 --> 00:13:55,094
runways with high pressure water,

364
00:13:55,094 --> 00:13:57,320
because too much of it makes the

365
00:13:57,320 --> 00:13:59,059
runway slippery when wet.

366
00:13:59,410 --> 00:14:02,187
That spin-up moment is also the biggest

367
00:14:02,187 --> 00:14:04,181
test for the tire structure.

368
00:14:04,361 --> 00:14:07,327
The sudden twist from standing still to

369
00:14:07,327 --> 00:14:09,989
full speed pulls hard on the rubber

370
00:14:09,989 --> 00:14:11,130
and the casing.

371
00:14:11,310 --> 00:14:14,240
Engineers design tires and wheels for it,

372
00:14:14,240 --> 00:14:16,670
and the landing gear itself has to

373
00:14:16,670 --> 00:14:19,314
handle the backward drag force as the

374
00:14:19,314 --> 00:14:20,958
wheels grab the runway.

375
00:14:21,138 --> 00:14:23,710
Watch the gear in slow motion and

376
00:14:23,710 --> 00:14:26,048
you can see the whole leg flex

377
00:14:26,048 --> 00:14:27,918
backward for an instant.

378
00:14:28,268 --> 00:14:30,845
Why are there red ribbons hanging from

379
00:14:30,845 --> 00:14:33,422
the landing gear of a parked airplane?

380
00:14:33,602 --> 00:14:35,901
They are attached to gear pins,

381
00:14:35,901 --> 00:14:38,941
steel pins pushed into the gear mechanism

382
00:14:38,941 --> 00:14:41,387
to lock it physically in the down

383
00:14:41,387 --> 00:14:42,054
position.

384
00:14:42,234 --> 00:14:45,502
A parked airplane has no hydraulic pressure,

385
00:14:45,502 --> 00:14:48,770
and someone could accidentally move the gear

386
00:14:48,770 --> 00:14:50,330
lever in the cockpit,

387
00:14:50,330 --> 00:14:53,078
or a mechanic could trigger a system.

388
00:14:53,258 --> 00:14:56,568
The pin makes it mechanically impossible for

389
00:14:56,568 --> 00:14:58,675
the gear to fold up and drop

390
00:14:58,675 --> 00:15:00,630
the airplane on its belly.

391
00:15:00,981 --> 00:15:03,763
The red flag says remove before flight,

392
00:15:03,763 --> 00:15:05,903
and that is exactly the point.

393
00:15:06,083 --> 00:15:08,451
A pin left in place means the

394
00:15:08,451 --> 00:15:11,226
gear cannot retract after takeoff.

395
00:15:11,406 --> 00:15:13,150
So the ribbon is bright,

396
00:15:13,150 --> 00:15:15,982
long, and impossible to miss during the

397
00:15:15,982 --> 00:15:17,653
walk around inspection.

398
00:15:17,833 --> 00:15:20,639
The same red flags are used on

399
00:15:20,639 --> 00:15:23,258
pitot covers, engine covers,

400
00:15:23,258 --> 00:15:25,971
and weapons on military jets.

401
00:15:26,151 --> 00:15:29,042
It is one of the simplest safety

402
00:15:29,042 --> 00:15:30,849
devices in aviation,

403
00:15:30,849 --> 00:15:33,289
a piece of cloth that says,

404
00:15:33,289 --> 00:15:34,644
in big letters,

405
00:15:34,644 --> 00:15:36,089
don't forget me.

406
00:15:36,440 --> 00:15:38,859
Gear pins are part of a bigger

407
00:15:38,859 --> 00:15:39,505
culture.

408
00:15:39,685 --> 00:15:41,293
Before every flight,

409
00:15:41,293 --> 00:15:44,189
a pilot or mechanic walks around the

410
00:15:44,189 --> 00:15:47,808
airplane looking for anything that should not

411
00:15:47,808 --> 00:15:49,175
be there, covers,

412
00:15:49,175 --> 00:15:50,784
pins, leaks, damage.

413
00:15:50,964 --> 00:15:53,002
It sounds old fashioned,

414
00:15:53,002 --> 00:15:56,228
but it catches problems that no sensor

415
00:15:56,228 --> 00:15:56,653
sees.

416
00:15:56,833 --> 00:15:59,691
The red flags exist because the walk

417
00:15:59,691 --> 00:16:00,803
around exists.

418
00:16:00,983 --> 00:16:02,667
One makes the other work.

419
00:16:03,017 --> 00:16:06,021
What happens if the hydraulic system fails

420
00:16:06,021 --> 00:16:08,452
and the landing gear will not come

421
00:16:08,452 --> 00:16:08,810
down?

422
00:16:08,990 --> 00:16:12,496
Pilots have a backup that needs almost

423
00:16:12,496 --> 00:16:14,156
no power, gravity.

424
00:16:14,336 --> 00:16:15,776
On most airliners,

425
00:16:15,776 --> 00:16:18,416
a handle or switch in the cockpit

426
00:16:18,416 --> 00:16:21,376
releases the locks that hold the gear

427
00:16:21,376 --> 00:16:21,616
up.

428
00:16:21,796 --> 00:16:24,284
The heavy gear simply falls out of

429
00:16:24,284 --> 00:16:25,455
the wheel wells,

430
00:16:25,455 --> 00:16:27,724
pushed by its own weight and by

431
00:16:27,724 --> 00:16:29,262
the air rushing past,

432
00:16:29,262 --> 00:16:31,897
until it locks in the down position.

433
00:16:32,077 --> 00:16:34,850
It is called a gravity extension,

434
00:16:34,850 --> 00:16:35,943
or free fall.

435
00:16:36,293 --> 00:16:39,279
It is tested on certification flights,

436
00:16:39,279 --> 00:16:42,423
and pilots practice it in the simulator.

437
00:16:42,603 --> 00:16:45,116
It works so well that true belly

438
00:16:45,116 --> 00:16:47,943
landings of airliners are very rare.

439
00:16:48,123 --> 00:16:50,523
When the gear really will not come

440
00:16:50,523 --> 00:16:53,416
down, it is usually because something has

441
00:16:53,416 --> 00:16:56,169
jammed, not because of a power failure.

442
00:16:56,349 --> 00:16:59,272
Simple physics, gravity and airflow,

443
00:16:59,272 --> 00:17:01,545
is the last line of defense,

444
00:17:01,545 --> 00:17:04,143
because it is the one thing that

445
00:17:04,143 --> 00:17:06,740
never runs out on an airplane in

446
00:17:06,740 --> 00:17:07,309
flight.

447
00:17:07,659 --> 00:17:09,560
The gear also has a way to

448
00:17:09,560 --> 00:17:12,047
tell the pilots it is really down.

449
00:17:12,227 --> 00:17:15,067
Three green lights in the cockpit,

450
00:17:15,067 --> 00:17:16,904
one for each gear leg,

451
00:17:16,904 --> 00:17:19,410
light up only when each one is

452
00:17:19,410 --> 00:17:19,994
locked.

453
00:17:20,174 --> 00:17:22,723
If a light stays red or dark,

454
00:17:22,723 --> 00:17:25,974
pilots can fly past the control tower

455
00:17:25,974 --> 00:17:28,083
so controllers can look,

456
00:17:28,083 --> 00:17:30,983
or use cameras on some airplanes.

457
00:17:31,163 --> 00:17:33,688
Three green is one of the most

458
00:17:33,688 --> 00:17:36,213
reassuring sights in aviation.

459
00:17:36,564 --> 00:17:37,992
So here are the ten.

460
00:17:38,172 --> 00:17:40,034
Nitrogen in the tires,

461
00:17:40,034 --> 00:17:43,081
because of a tire that exploded with

462
00:17:43,081 --> 00:17:43,419
air.

463
00:17:43,599 --> 00:17:46,154
Plugs that melt so the tire goes

464
00:17:46,154 --> 00:17:48,150
flat instead of bursting.

465
00:17:48,330 --> 00:17:50,269
Straight grooves for water.

466
00:17:50,449 --> 00:17:53,979
A shock absorber full of oil and

467
00:17:53,979 --> 00:17:54,420
gas.

468
00:17:54,600 --> 00:17:57,235
Scissors that keep the wheels straight.

469
00:17:57,415 --> 00:17:59,819
Brakes tested until they glow.

470
00:17:59,999 --> 00:18:03,354
Anti-lock brakes that were invented for jets.

471
00:18:03,534 --> 00:18:06,374
A puff of smoke at every touchdown,

472
00:18:06,374 --> 00:18:08,890
red flags that must be removed,

473
00:18:08,890 --> 00:18:11,487
and gravity as the final backup.

474
00:18:11,837 --> 00:18:14,453
Landing gear looks like a few wheels

475
00:18:14,453 --> 00:18:15,180
on sticks.

476
00:18:15,360 --> 00:18:17,468
It is really one of the most

477
00:18:17,468 --> 00:18:19,575
tested parts of an airplane,

478
00:18:19,575 --> 00:18:21,757
shaped by decades of lessons.

479
00:18:21,937 --> 00:18:25,033
If you want more machines explained part

480
00:18:25,033 --> 00:18:27,975
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

481
00:18:28,155 --> 00:18:30,367
And tell us in the comments which

482
00:18:30,367 --> 00:18:32,579
machine you want us to take apart

483
00:18:32,579 --> 00:18:32,915
next.
